1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:03,000
Uh, module three, video 3.6, the review.

2
00:00:03,740 --> 00:00:06,420
Three people, every open line given a name.

3
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:16,120
Right. And, uh, the core of it is that the owner, the builder, and the person who will live with the result agree on the screens, so absolutely nothing leaves the room unowned.

4
00:00:16,460 --> 00:00:19,920
Which is so crucial, right? Yeah. I mean, think about it in your ministry.

5
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:24,900
Why does a review that ends with, uh, "We will look into it," decide nothing for your ministry?

6
00:00:25,220 --> 00:00:28,420
Well, because the review you convene ends with a strict record.

7
00:00:28,620 --> 00:00:33,720
Right. You need every open question, uh, the person who owns it, and the exact date it is due.

8
00:00:34,020 --> 00:00:35,520
Yeah, without that, things just drift.

9
00:00:36,360 --> 00:00:39,260
So who exactly sits in the three seats at this review?

10
00:00:39,700 --> 00:00:42,240
Let's, um, let's use the example from Progressa.

11
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:43,720
Ah, right, Progressa,

12
00:00:44,520 --> 00:00:47,160
which is a fictional country, just to clarify for everyone.

13
00:00:47,380 --> 00:00:48,420
Yes, purely fictional.

14
00:00:49,100 --> 00:00:54,240
But in their example, looking at the, uh, registration desk review, what they call PHEQA,

15
00:00:55,060 --> 00:00:57,180
we number the seats allowed to ensure everyone is there.

16
00:00:57,680 --> 00:00:58,760
Okay, so seat one?

17
00:00:59,540 --> 00:01:00,860
Seat one is the owner,

18
00:01:01,480 --> 00:01:04,120
and the owner answers for what the documents actually say.

19
00:01:04,780 --> 00:01:05,120
Got it.

20
00:01:05,800 --> 00:01:06,360
And seat two?

21
00:01:07,160 --> 00:01:10,240
Two is the builder. The builder says whether, um,

22
00:01:10,840 --> 00:01:13,660
whether he can build it without asking anybody anything.

23
00:01:14,080 --> 00:01:15,900
Wow, without asking anything at all.

24
00:01:16,300 --> 00:01:18,060
Exactly. It has to be that clear.

25
00:01:18,700 --> 00:01:21,720
And then three is the person who will live with the result.

26
00:01:22,440 --> 00:01:26,180
She says whether the screens are actually her work as she does it.

27
00:01:26,920 --> 00:01:30,940
Okay, so applied to the PHEQA example, who-

28
00:01:31,240 --> 00:01:33,040
Who are those specific people? Yeah, exactly.

29
00:01:33,840 --> 00:01:35,880
Right. So at PHEQA,

30
00:01:36,920 --> 00:01:42,520
those three are the supplier's analyst, the supplier's builder, and the head of the registration desk.

31
00:01:43,180 --> 00:01:43,520
Okay.

32
00:01:44,140 --> 00:01:45,880
And what about the person running the meeting?

33
00:01:46,520 --> 00:01:47,120
The convener.

34
00:01:47,980 --> 00:01:50,020
They just accept the record. They take no seat.

35
00:01:50,540 --> 00:01:51,200
Ah, okay.

36
00:01:52,120 --> 00:01:56,500
So, um, what are the four questions the convener opens with before the walkthrough even begins?

37
00:01:57,260 --> 00:01:58,960
Well, the convener numbers them aloud.

38
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:00,040
Number one,

39
00:02:00,700 --> 00:02:04,540
does every screen serve a step or a failure, and is everyone served?

40
00:02:05,220 --> 00:02:05,400
Right.

41
00:02:06,080 --> 00:02:08,759
Two, does every value say where it comes from?

42
00:02:09,160 --> 00:02:11,520
Mm-hmm, essential for clean data in your program.

43
00:02:11,620 --> 00:02:12,040
Definitely.

44
00:02:12,660 --> 00:02:12,900
Three,

45
00:02:13,660 --> 00:02:17,480
could the builder build each screen without asking anybody anything?

46
00:02:17,940 --> 00:02:19,360
There's that strict builder rule again.

47
00:02:19,660 --> 00:02:24,480
Yep. And four, would the person who will use it recognize it as her work?

48
00:02:25,140 --> 00:02:27,020
Okay, and once those four questions are out there...

49
00:02:27,540 --> 00:02:31,500
Then the person from the desk clicks through the walkthrough, literally page by page.

50
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:36,960
Okay, so going back to Progressa. In Progressa, the fictional country of these examples,

51
00:02:37,620 --> 00:02:41,860
what were the five open lines recorded during PHEQA's one-hour review?

52
00:02:42,320 --> 00:02:46,120
Well, we number the five lines, and we state the owner and the date for each one.

53
00:02:47,060 --> 00:02:47,780
Okay, let's hear them.

54
00:02:48,260 --> 00:02:55,360
One, show the fee on S2 as well as S4. That one is owned by the supplier's analyst, due 26 November.

55
00:02:55,940 --> 00:02:57,020
Got it, S2 and S4.

56
00:02:57,780 --> 00:02:57,960
Two,

57
00:02:58,740 --> 00:03:01,760
is a name held by a canceled institution still held?

58
00:03:02,660 --> 00:03:05,160
Owned by the registrar, due 3 December. Right.

59
00:03:05,900 --> 00:03:06,140
Three,

60
00:03:06,840 --> 00:03:08,960
the governing board, one member to align.

61
00:03:09,860 --> 00:03:12,160
Owned by the supplier's analyst, due 26 November.

62
00:03:12,620 --> 00:03:12,880
Mm-hmm.

63
00:03:13,620 --> 00:03:13,860
Four,

64
00:03:14,640 --> 00:03:18,020
what may the screen say when PNIA does not answer?

65
00:03:18,840 --> 00:03:22,200
That one is owned by the head of the ICT unit, due 3rd December.

66
00:03:23,020 --> 00:03:23,980
Okay, and the last one.

67
00:03:24,420 --> 00:03:27,620
Five, is a printed confirmation asked for anywhere?

68
00:03:28,160 --> 00:03:30,300
Owned by the registrar, due 26 November.

69
00:03:30,660 --> 00:03:36,980
So after the meeting, how is each answer actually written into the document that owns the fact, you know, before the screens are accepted?

70
00:03:37,280 --> 00:03:39,640
Well, the owner changes the screens, first off.

71
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:41,480
Then the walkthrough is produced again.

72
00:03:41,720 --> 00:03:42,920
Ah, an iterative process.

73
00:03:43,220 --> 00:03:48,020
Right, and the answers go into shared lists, the architecture or the register of requirements.

74
00:03:48,460 --> 00:03:49,740
And when are they finally accepted?

75
00:03:50,040 --> 00:03:54,560
Screens are accepted when every line is closed or, um, allowed to stay open with its owner.

76
00:03:55,120 --> 00:04:01,760
Okay, so looking at the broader picture, how does the reference architecture for public administration treat this review as a stage gate?

77
00:04:02,140 --> 00:04:07,060
Well, it recommends robust project governance frameworks and stage gate models with agile iterations.

78
00:04:07,280 --> 00:04:09,900
Right. The review is that gate for one goal.

79
00:04:10,340 --> 00:04:14,680
A record with no open lines usually means the review was not held or nobody spoke.

80
00:04:14,840 --> 00:04:15,840
Which is a huge red flag.

81
00:04:16,100 --> 00:04:21,200
Exactly. It risks systems becoming duplicated, siloed, and ultimately costly to change.

82
00:04:22,160 --> 00:04:25,240
Seat the owner, the builder, and the person who will use the service.

83
00:04:26,040 --> 00:04:29,640
End with every open line written down, each with a name and a date.

84
00:04:30,100 --> 00:04:31,520
Sources are in the video description.
